WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.313
(Putting on boots)

00:00:05.313 --> 00:00:08.700
Let’s… go for a walk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:14.751
And let’s slow things down a bit. And take notice&nbsp;of the world right beneath our feet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.751 --> 00:00:22.260
In these miniature environments, vast dramas unfold,&nbsp;
often without us ever realizing it. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:28.320
if we could become part of these sagas? In&nbsp;
the Pikmin series, you play as a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.260
under two centimeters tall, and experience&nbsp;
a tiny yet rich ecosystem from the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:40.260
level. And though the Pikmin universe is more&nbsp;
stylized than some, its surprisingly complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:46.560
lore makes it ideal for study. So, let’s shrink&nbsp;
down, and see the world from a new perspective…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:57.000
In a flash of light, our ship arrives planetside.&nbsp;
This is world PNF-404 — though we would know it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.060
planet Earth. But this is not the Earth of&nbsp;
today. Humanity is gone, and in its place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.420
strange new orders of life have emerged.&nbsp;
We are a very tiny explorer from a distant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:16.140
planet. It is our job to catalogue this world —&nbsp;
a daunting task, considering its many dangers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:23.340
Fortunately, we are not alone in our&nbsp;
endeavor. This is a Pikmin. A Red Pikmin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.760
specifically. These friendly local lifeforms will&nbsp;
help us uncover the mysteries of this planet. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:36.600
what are Pikmin? It’s a question we can’t answer&nbsp;
right away, for their biology is so bizarre, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.720
will take time to formulate a theory. Let’s start&nbsp;
with what we do know about Pikmin. Pikmin work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.840
together. Like ants or termites, they’re eusocial&nbsp;
— able to divide up their labor to benefit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.540
whole. While one group of Pikmin is defending from&nbsp;
a predator, another might be collecting resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:01.080
or even building a simple structure. We also&nbsp;
know that Pikmin are unusually strong, able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.720
to carry objects much larger than their bodies.&nbsp;
Ants are capable of similar feats of impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.180
strength relative to their size. Where Pikmin&nbsp;
differ from familiar forms of life is in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:19.500
reproduction. Pikmin are ‘born’ from a strange,&nbsp;
globular plant nicknamed an Onion. The Onion will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.920
periodically release seeds into the air, which&nbsp;
develop underground into adult Pikmin. It’s such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.020
an unusual process that we’ll need to know more&nbsp;
about their world before we can understand it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:39.240
The first region we’ve landed in is the&nbsp;
Sun-Speckled Terrace. Lit by gentle, dappled rays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:46.620
this area seems peaceful at a glance. But there&nbsp;
are many hungry mouths here. These are Bulborbs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:53.400
A member of the Grub-Dog family, Bulborbs come&nbsp;
in all shapes and sizes, but are united in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.280
bottomless appetites. One of the most successful&nbsp;
orders on the planet, Bulborbs have conquered all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.340
manner of environments, and filled all manner&nbsp;
of niches. The peculiar Whiptongue Bulborb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.460
for example, has a feeding method similar to an&nbsp;
anteater — able to slurp up multiple targets with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:17.700
its long, sticky tongue. We are fortunate to have&nbsp;
Pikmin on our side to help keep these beasts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:25.500
bay. But Bulborbs are far from the only danger.&nbsp;
These are Fiery Blowhogs: creatures with poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.980
manners and hot tempers. When threatened, they can&nbsp;
expel a volatile phosphorus compound from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.580
snouts, which combusts immediately upon contact&nbsp;
with air. This is where Red Pikmin come in handy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.560
as flame-proof cellulose in their skin&nbsp;
makes them heat-resistant. All the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:51.420
it’s best we give Blowhogs… a wide berth. More&nbsp;
extreme lifeforms await us underground. In the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:57.780
caverns beneath the terrace, unique subterranean&nbsp;
species have emerged, like the Snowy Blowhogs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:03.300
Unlike their fiery relatives, they prefer to&nbsp;
cool things down instead of heating them up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:10.200
able to expel a compound similar to dry ice from&nbsp;
an internal cold sac. Tusked Blowhogs forgo the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.020
complex chemistry of their siblings altogether, in&nbsp;
favor of the simpler strategy of running headfirst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:22.500
into prey. These caves are also home to the&nbsp;
unpleasant Sheargrubs, creatures that emerge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:28.200
from the ground in droves. Like Pikmin, there are&nbsp;
many specialized types of Sheargrubs — with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.980
possessing wings, and others long snouts ideal&nbsp;
for spearing prey. And they have a nasty habit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:41.400
swarming. To a human sized lifeform, these insects&nbsp;
would be little threat. But this is a world for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:47.640
the bugs now. Back on the surface, Flint Beetles&nbsp;
— descendants of modern beetles — and Spectralids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:53.820
– descendants of butterflies, thrive in large&nbsp;
numbers. From our new, scaled-down perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:05:01.020
these familiar species have a newfound grandeur.&nbsp;
It is often our viewpoint that defines our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:07.020
surroundings. The Sun-Speckled Terrace seems to be&nbsp;
the remains of a small garden, but navigating it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:13.080
feels like crossing a vast, mountainous terrain.&nbsp;
It made me think more about my own perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.380
…There’s a creek near the place where I grew&nbsp;
up. I went for a walk there the other day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:26.160
and as I walked, I stopped at random points and&nbsp;
stuck my camera on the ground. It was… awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.820
at first. But it made me realize something.&nbsp;
There are… a lot of bugs out there. And yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.640
everyone knows there’s lots of bugs out there — I&nbsp;
knew there’s lots of bugs out there. And yet… it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.680
still felt so different to actually see them all.&nbsp;
I passed so many bugs in the first few minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:50.760
it made me wonder how many hidden worlds I walk&nbsp;
past every day… Like the Blossoming Arcadia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.740
another microecosystem in Pikmin. It appears&nbsp;
to be the remains of an old playground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:01.980
but like the Terrace, this ordinary place&nbsp;
becomes a perilous expanse due to our tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:08.340
lens. Fortunately, the Yellow Pikmin are here&nbsp;
to give us a helping hand. Their bodies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.620
naturally resistant to electric currents, able&nbsp;
to withstand shocks like certain species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:19.200
catfish. And it’s a good thing too, because&nbsp;
many species in the Arcadia pack a serious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:26.220
electrical punch. This unusual specimen is a&nbsp;
Bearded Amprat. Though nonthreatening when calm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.320
these territorial creatures will sometimes&nbsp;
rub the hair on their face to generate a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:37.080
formidable electric field. The Yellow Pikmin,&nbsp;
however, are unimpressed by the voltage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.620
Leaves fall in more peaceful regions of the&nbsp;
playground. But bump into certain leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:49.740
and you might be surprised. These are Skitter&nbsp;
Leaves, creatures that panic and live up to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:55.680
name when poked. Because they often camouflage in&nbsp;
a group, waking one Skitter Leaf can start a chain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:01.860
reaction that causes the entire colony to panic.&nbsp;
Perhaps it’s kindest to simply watch our step.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:09.120
A species that will not extend this courtesy&nbsp;
to us is the Yellow Wollyhop. A rather rotund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:14.580
member of the Amphituber family, they possess&nbsp;
the rather rude instinct to crush smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:20.700
lifeforms. We’ll have to move fast to stay&nbsp;
alive. Fortunately, we can catch a ride on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:27.240
a furry companion. This is Oatchi. A loyal canine&nbsp;
seemingly distantly related to the dogs of Earth;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:33.600
we don’t know much about his biology. But I&nbsp;
really like Oatchi. Oatchi is a very good boy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.680
I’m less fond of the Waddlequaff. These feathered&nbsp;
lifeforms use their overdeveloped respiratory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.960
muscles to suck in large volumes of air through&nbsp;
their beaks. An effective method of feeding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:51.480
if a frustrating one to be on the other end&nbsp;
of. But it’s better than facing down the spear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.600
of a Joustmite. A burrowing insect, Joustmites&nbsp;
are quite proficient at spearing anything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:03.300
disturbs their slumber clean through — including&nbsp;
multiple targets should the need arise. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:08.940
behavior similar to the Swooping Snitchbug, who&nbsp;
will snatch up multiple Pikmin and burrow them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:14.040
back in the ground. And if these fluttering pests&nbsp;
aren’t enough to demonstrate the newfound might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:20.280
of insects, the bioelectric Anode Beetles of the&nbsp;
caves certainly are. These insects can connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:26.580
with each other to create a discharge too powerful&nbsp;
to withstand. Only our trusty Yellow Pikmin are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:33.000
up for the task. But there are some challenges&nbsp;
even Yellow Pikmin are not up for. These hopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.840
fledglings are Downy Snagrets, and probably don’t&nbsp;
seem like a significant threat. But they are only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:47.460
babies — and their parent is close by. From the&nbsp;
ground emerges a colossal Burrowing Snagret. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:53.040
bird has converged upon the niche of an ambush&nbsp;
predator. Their mobility and sheer size make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:59.400
them the tyrant rulers of the Blossoming Arcadia.&nbsp;
Perhaps this region simply isn’t worth the risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:05.400
Maybe we’ll have better luck on the coast.&nbsp;
Based on the name alone, the Serene Shores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:11.940
seem quite peaceful. There’s just one problem.&nbsp;
Ordinary Pikmin can’t swim. We’re going to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.160
some kind of specialist. The Blue Pikmin&nbsp;
will be the perfect lifeguards. Thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:22.440
a mouth-like gill membrane, these Pikmin can&nbsp;
breathe underwater — and are ready to tussle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.780
with all manner of aquatic life. They’re&nbsp;
the ideal allies for this watery ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:34.620
Skimming on top of the water is a graceful&nbsp;
Skeeterskate. Likely descended from pond skaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:40.860
Skeeterskates use hydrophobic hairs on their feet&nbsp;
to remain on top of the water. They store water in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:47.640
a sac atop their heads, which they’ll regurgitate&nbsp;
if we get too close. But it’s better to be spit at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:54.000
than eaten whole. Water Dumples are unassuming&nbsp;
purple blobs with ravenous appetites. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:10:00.480
behavior makes more sense when you consider their&nbsp;
closest relative is the terrestrial Bulborb. Yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.360
their appetite seems almost quaint compared to the&nbsp;
Peckish Aristocrab. While these crustaceans begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:13.140
their life as tiny nuisances, they soon grow into&nbsp;
full sized, intertidal terrors. An unfortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:19.260
creature for those who enjoy long walks on the&nbsp;
beach. And Aristocrabs aren’t the only crustacean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:24.660
to look out for. Hermit Crawmads are ambush&nbsp;
hunters that will rush at sources of movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:30.600
from their burrow, before awkwardly wobbling back&nbsp;
underground. A descendent of the hermit crab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:36.540
Crawmads have lost their shells entirely, and as&nbsp;
a result are not the bravest creatures here on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.780
coast. If a Crawmad fails in their initial attack,&nbsp;
they will often shamefully scuttle away in defeat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:48.960
Other ambush predators have a less subtle,&nbsp;
but far more destructive approach. Armored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.180
Cannon Larva will regurgitate large stones from&nbsp;
their gullet to flatten enemies. They feed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.180 --> 00:11:00.240
minerals to support the bacteria in their gut — a&nbsp;
behavior analogous to certain types of shipworms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:06.300
that consume limestone for the same reason. A&nbsp;
questionable diet, but an unquestionably effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:14.100
offensive tactic. Not every animal on the Serene&nbsp;
Shores is quite so hostile. Puckering Blinnows are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:20.100
relatively peaceful creatures that swim in schools&nbsp;
beneath the surface. Pearly Clamclamps are another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:26.040
seemingly calm semi-aquatic species. But trigger&nbsp;
their snapping mechanism, and you’ll find there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:31.980
more to these clams than meets the eye. Clamclamps&nbsp;
are the ultimate patient hunters, using their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:37.920
pearls as bait to ensnare unlucky prey. They’re&nbsp;
proof some treasures… just aren’t worth it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:43.980
There’s a region of the Serene Shores that&nbsp;
appears to be an abandoned aquarium. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:48.240
you can encounter Jellyfloats, strange&nbsp;
floating lifeforms that can hover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.240
likely due to some internal gas bladder. Yet while&nbsp;
one’s focus should definitely remain on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:00.360
giant floating predators, when I encounter them, I&nbsp;
keep thinking about the surrounding aquarium, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:06.720
uncanny it is that an ecosystem has formed around&nbsp;
this abandoned place. I have a similar experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:13.260
facing down the Crusted Rumpup. The apex predator&nbsp;
of its environment, this giant has poor eyesight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:19.500
and charges blindly at any potential meal. Not&nbsp;
a creature to face while distracted. Yet while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:25.860
evading this goliath, I found myself once again&nbsp;
transfixed by the surroundings. The Rumpup seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:31.560
to make its home in an abandoned sandcastle.&nbsp;
Like the aquarium, here, the remnants of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:36.900
past define the present. The same can be said&nbsp;
for most of the environments in this game — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:42.000
shape of each ecosystem determined by junk&nbsp;
humanity left behind without a second thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:49.080
The landscape of the creek from my childhood is&nbsp;
marked by similar relics. Plastic caps and broken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:54.420
bottles lie on the banks. I did my best to clean&nbsp;
up the area, although more trash will certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.420 --> 00:13:00.780
come. The creek itself isn’t even a natural stream&nbsp;
— it’s all runoff from a drainage pipe. Human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:06.600
overflow changes the shape of micro-environments&nbsp;
like this one all the time, despite most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:13.740
not even knowing they exist. Nowhere in Pikmin&nbsp;
is humanity’s influence more present than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:19.500
next region, which takes place entirely within&nbsp;
an abandoned human home — a unique ecosystem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:26.100
with unique life. The mighty Purple Pikmin are&nbsp;
going to be of particular help here, as man-made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:32.280
obstacles can be crushed under their considerable&nbsp;
bulk. On the floor of the household, strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:38.640
creatures teeter about. These oddities are called&nbsp;
Breadbugs, and live up to their names with bodies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:46.080
that resemble, well, bread. Breadbugs can be seen&nbsp;
dragging shiny objects to safe locations, perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:52.140
due to some nesting instinct. The real mystery,&nbsp;
however, is why any species would evolve to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:57.960
look like a common food item? The Giant Breadbug&nbsp;
subspecies looks even more like a delectable loaf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:03.840
deepening the mystery. Considering that camouflage&nbsp;
is typically a strategy to avoid getting eaten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:08.280
it is probable that the Breadbugs&nbsp;
resemblance to food is just a coincidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:15.840
Another culinary creature oozes across the&nbsp;
floor. The Sunsquish resembles a fried egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:22.020
yet is actually a kind of protochordate, a type&nbsp;
of invertebrate. Though carnivorous, this is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:27.960
egg that’s not too difficult to scramble. But&nbsp;
you’re the one more likely to get scrambled if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:34.020
you start a fight with a Mammoth Snootwacker.&nbsp;
This species sports a tough exterior shell to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:39.480
deter predators. But if they still don’t get the&nbsp;
message, the Snootwacker can wind up their trunks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:46.680
for a devastating strike. When a species asks for&nbsp;
privacy, it’s best to respect their wishes. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:52.560
plumbing systems below this house, a secondary&nbsp;
ecosystem thrives. This environment is home to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:59.400
creatures like the Pricklepuff, a pufferfish-like&nbsp;
animal with toxic spines. Luckily, Ice Pikmin can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:04.980
help keep us from needing to get our feet wet.&nbsp;
These Pikmin can freeze bodies of water, creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:10.740
a walkable surface. In-game scientists assume th&nbsp;
ese creatures are made entirely of ice. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:16.020
it’s more likely they have biological components&nbsp;
that let them expel a freezing chemical mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:21.600
much like a Snowy Blowhog, although we still need&nbsp;
to learn more about Pikmin in general to formulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:28.500
a proper hypothesis regarding their biology. With&nbsp;
Ice Pikmin in hand, we’ll make the acquaintance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:35.400
the Hydro Dweevil. These crab-like insects have a&nbsp;
natural instinct to hide under human objects. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:41.640
emergence of such a behavior across all Dweevil&nbsp;
subspecies speaks to the impact humanity’s debris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:48.060
has had on evolutionary processes. But other&nbsp;
creatures carry things for a more sinister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:54.360
reason. The Gildemandwee possess a sweet-smelling,&nbsp;
crystalline growth on their back to attract prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.360 --> 00:16:00.480
And while juvenile Gildemandwees have a somewhat&nbsp;
limited appetite, the adult Gildemander is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:05.880
prepared to ask for seconds. The vast growths on&nbsp;
their back combined with the angler-fish lights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:10.440
on their heads mean that even the wariest&nbsp;
prey can be pulled in by their display.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:17.160
Yet there is one species so wary, not even a&nbsp;
Gildemander could entice it. Although that doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:22.800
mean this cautious creature is defenseless. The&nbsp;
Scorchcake is one of the stranger lifeforms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:27.780
the Pikmin world. These molten disks might&nbsp;
not look like an animal at all, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:32.820
are actually a type of mollusk — a connection&nbsp;
that is more readily apparent when they extend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:38.940
their periscopic stalk eyes. Scorchcakes create&nbsp;
bursts of fire through oils secreted from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:45.420
shells — a strategy similar to the Pyroclasmic&nbsp;
Slooch, a species of terrestrial snail that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:50.880
might be the Scorchcake’s close relative. Though&nbsp;
Sloochs have abandoned the shells of their cousin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:57.600
they flash bright colors to ward off any predator&nbsp;
not deterred by their scorching trail. Perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:03.900
the most well defended creature of all is the&nbsp;
Porquillion. These spiny goliaths are among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:10.620
most formidable creatures in this entire region,&nbsp;
able to fire off spines at an alarming rate. If a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:17.160
Porquillion runs out, however, they must retreat&nbsp;
until their spikes are able to regrow. Such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:23.580
process expends considerable energy, and means the&nbsp;
Porquillion is always searching for its next meal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:30.000
Since we don’t want that to be us, we’ll try&nbsp;
our luck in the backyard, a region known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.300
Giant’s Hearth. And right away, it’s clear that&nbsp;
this area is the wrong locale to seek sanctuary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:42.420
Bulbears are Bulborb relatives that are even more&nbsp;
aggressive than their cousins. We’ll need some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:48.720
particularly tough Pikmin to brave this ecosystem.&nbsp;
And it’s difficult to get tougher than a Rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:55.020
Pikmin. These stony fellows are so strong, they&nbsp;
can burst directly through crystalline walls. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.020 --> 00:18:01.020
how does an organism made of stone function? In&nbsp;
game text suggests the Rock Pikmin’s hard exterior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:06.720
is actually just an outer shell of inorganic&nbsp;
material that a smaller lifeform exists within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:12.300
And when you consider that many modern organisms&nbsp;
have shells made of rock-hard calcium carbonate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:18.240
such an adaptation doesn’t seem so far-fetched.&nbsp;
A tough shell won’t do you much good against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:24.240
a Bloomcap Bloyster. These hungry mollusks&nbsp;
constantly probe with their feeding tendrils.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:30.480
Their smaller cousins, the Toady Bloysters,&nbsp;
seem less threatening at a glance. But looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:35.700
can be deceiving. This is one species that&nbsp;
has changed radically from its ancestors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:42.360
Other organisms have changed little over the&nbsp;
eons. Arachnodes are spider descendants that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:47.820
remain just as skilled as their ancestors,&nbsp;
able to sense food at any point in their webs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:55.380
Underground, little understood arthropods called&nbsp;
Shutterchucks collect shiny crystals. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.380 --> 00:19:00.960
crystals have useful properties, and Winged Pikmin&nbsp;
will help us transport them. They may be small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:07.560
but Winged Pikmin are deceptively strong. This&nbsp;
doesn’t seem impossible considering bees can haul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:13.440
up to 91 percent of their bodyweight in nectar&nbsp;
alone — yet it does show that the world of Pikmin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:20.220
still holds many secrets. Deeper underground, we&nbsp;
can at least answer the mystery of where Bulborbs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:26.400
come from. They appear to begin their life as tiny&nbsp;
larvae, utterly defenseless without an adult. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:33.960
an adult is close by. This is an Empress Bulbax,&nbsp;
a Grub-Dog with a massively enlarged egg sac for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:39.600
bearing children like the queen of an ant colony.&nbsp;
When threatened, this monarch will try to crush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:45.600
opponents with its sheer bulk — rolling directly&nbsp;
over them. But left to her own devices, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:52.320
Empress Bulbax prefers to peacefully snooze. In&nbsp;
fact, most of the creatures in this game seem calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:59.520
until the player stumbles by. …I loved looking&nbsp;
under rocks as a child, getting a glimpse of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:05.880
insects in their natural state. Except… I wasn’t&nbsp;
seeing them in their natural state, not really — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:10.920
was seeing their panic mode after their home had&nbsp;
been disrupted. To get nature to do what I wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:17.160
I acted less like an observer, and more like an&nbsp;
instigator. And the same thing happens when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:22.140
play Pikmin. You might have noticed throughout&nbsp;
this video that lots of creatures require a treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:27.360
to motivate them into doing something interesting&nbsp;
for the camera. You’re not really connecting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:34.500
the ecosystem; you’re more pushing it into doing&nbsp;
what you want. Of course, sometimes, the ecosystem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:40.260
pushes back. Explore at night, and we’ll find&nbsp;
familiar locations made eerie and unsettling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:45.300
and animals becoming more aggressive. Your&nbsp;
only hope is to make an alliance with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:51.480
mysterious Glow Pikmin, who seem to be ghosts.&nbsp;
Like, the in-game encyclopedia implies they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:56.880
straight up ghosts. So, I don’t know how their&nbsp;
biology works, I got nothing. The night sections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:01.560
are another grave reminder that there’s still&nbsp;
much about this ecosystem we don’t understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:08.220
I found footprints by the creek the last time I&nbsp;
went. And while this made me feel like a tracker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:13.920
the truth is I don’t know what kind of animal they&nbsp;
belong to. I feel the same way in Pikmin when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:18.720
encounter something like the Mamuta — a strange,&nbsp;
hairy creature that doesn’t fit with the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:23.880
animals I’ve encountered. Other than the fact it&nbsp;
likes to plant Pikmin, I know nothing about its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:31.920
biology or lineage. Perhaps the final region has&nbsp;
some answers. The Primordial Thicket is a vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:38.700
undergrowth teeming with life more dangerous than&nbsp;
any other area. Dense networks of fungi coat the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:45.060
forest floor, and thick clouds of spores can block&nbsp;
certain pathways. The tiny White Pikmin can help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:51.720
thanks to their natural immunity to the spores’&nbsp;
toxins. Indeed, these spores seem to be parasitic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:58.140
as they’ve overtaken the bodies of many animals&nbsp;
that live here. And this — at last — provides key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:04.140
potential insight into the mysteries of Pikmin&nbsp;
biology. Many of the animals in this forest seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:09.960
to be a combination of both fungus and animal.&nbsp;
In some cases, like that of the well-camouflaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:15.720
Startle Spore, the fungal parasites seem to&nbsp;
be an integral part of their biology. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:20.640
Startle Spore has a cousin, in the form of&nbsp;
the more plant-like Creeping Chrysanthemum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:26.460
And something about how the Chrysanthemum&nbsp;
emerges from the ground seems awfully familiar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:32.580
More on that in a moment… One species that seems&nbsp;
immune to the fungal network is the towering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:38.340
Grubchucker. These bipedal creatures snag prey&nbsp;
with the long reach of their pincers and toss them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:44.340
into the mouth on top of their heads. The food is&nbsp;
then digested slowly — a fate we want to avoid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:50.640
We must also avoid cloudy water, for the&nbsp;
Bogswallow lies in wait. Possessing six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:56.220
primitive eyes, these creatures seem to be&nbsp;
a holdover from a forgotten primeval age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.940 --> 00:23:02.700
Yet the last new creature we’ll visit today is far&nbsp;
more ancient, and mighty. It’s a Sovereign Bulbax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:08.640
the largest member of the Grub-Dog family.&nbsp;
These genuine titans snag food with their long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:15.600
sticky tongues, and can swallow up many unwary&nbsp;
Pikmin. Notably, the Sovereign Bulbax cultivate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:21.360
a rich carpet of plant matter on their backs that&nbsp;
helps them stay camouflaged under the ground. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:28.740
another example of plant and animal symbiosis that&nbsp;
might finally let us crack our big mystery… Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:35.160
all we’ve seen, the Pikmin may be . . . hybrids&nbsp;
of animals and plants — specifically, parasitic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:40.500
plants like the Creeping Chrysanthemum. There’s&nbsp;
evidence to support this, as some explorers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:46.380
turned into plant hybrids after exposure to an&nbsp;
onion. If this is correct, Pikmin have clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:51.960
evolved to become heavily intertwined with their&nbsp;
plant partners. Indeed, one in-game scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:57.600
proposes that Pikmin are entirely plants, and&nbsp;
evolved from root vegetables. And while this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:03.360
seems… less likely, as is often the case with&nbsp;
Pikmin, it’s impossible to say for certain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:10.800
Though the time has come to leave this particular&nbsp;
world behind, it’s worth reflecting on our new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:16.020
perspective and the mysteries that remain.&nbsp;
Pikmin isn’t a series that reveals all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:21.180
its secrets. Like a child turning over rocks,&nbsp;
you’ll always be an interloper to some extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:26.220
an outsider making assumptions. But it’s a&nbsp;
series that makes you think more about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:31.740
ecosystems just below you. And I’ve left plenty&nbsp;
of stones unturned and creatures undocumented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:38.460
if you’re interested in exploring this world&nbsp;
yourself. And as always, thanks for watching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:44.040
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your&nbsp;
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:49.380
the notification icon to stay up to date on&nbsp;
all things Curious. See you in the next video.

